{"notes": "Synthesizing the five rate-relevant drivers I have a mandate over. Inflation: headline CPI yoy has fallen to 2.97% (12m low) with 3m annualized headline at just 0.90% and core 3m annualized at 2.81%, both decelerating clearly from a year ago — disinflation is live and fresh (last print negative m/m). This argues for lower yields via the inflation channel. Labor tightness: unemployment has risen steadily to 4.10%, a 12m high, with positive change over 3/6/12m and a Sahm gap of +0.47pp — labor market is loosening (less tight), which is disinflationary/dovish for policy and pulls yields down. Term premium: DGS10 sits at 4.36, near the middle of its 12m range (range_position 0.51) but has ticked up over 21d (-0.25 actually, wait: dgs10_change_21d is -0.25pp, i.e., yields fell over the last month) while up over 63d/252d — so recent momentum is downward even as the longer trend has been up; this is a mixed/stale-ish signal, I weight the more recent -0.25pp move higher. Financial conditions: NFCI is near a 52w low, slightly easing (less tight) over 4w/13w, in the loose portion of its range — modestly consistent with easier conditions, which alone doesn't directly push yields but reduces the case for higher term premium. Balance sheet: assets continue to shrink (-53B 4w, -253B 13w, -3.39% 13w change), a clear ongoing runoff — QT continues, a mechanical upward pressure on yields via reduced Fed absorption of duration, but the direction of the driver itself (assets) is falling clearly.

Net picture: three of four rate-relevant fundamental drivers (inflation, labor tightness, term premium momentum) point toward lower yields, while balance-sheet runoff pushes the other way structurally but is a slower-moving, well-priced force. Financial conditions are neutral-to-slightly-easing. On balance, I lean toward modestly lower yields, sized down given the mixed balance-sheet signal and only middling term-premium clarity.</notes>
